['Here's a basic structure for `StateLoader` class that you can build on according to your specific requirements:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.exception.ExceptionUtils;
import org.slf4j.*;

public class StateLoader {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(StateLoader.class);

    private final ConfigurationManagementService configService;
    private final DependencyManagementSystem depManager;
    private ExecutorService executorService;

    public StateLoader(ConfigurationManagementService configService, DependencyManagementSystem depManager) {
        this.configService = configService;
        this.depManager = depManager;
        int availableProcessors = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();
        executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(availableProcessors);
    }

    public Future<MoSTState> loadStateAsync(String filePath) {
        return executorService.submit(() -> 
            retryOnTransientIO(() -> 
                validateAndLoad(filePath)));
    }

    private MoSTState validateAndLoad(String filePath) throws IOException, InvalidFileException, VersionMismatchException, SerializationIssueException {
        MoSTState state;

        try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            state = (MoSTState) in.readObject();

            String version = configService.getVersionForDependency("some_dependency");
            if (!version.equals(state.getRequiredVersion())) {
                throw new VersionMismatchException();
            }

            depManager.resolveDependencies(filePath);
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            logger.error("Serialization error", e);
            throw new SerializationIssueException(e);
        } catch (DependencyResolutionFailureException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to resolve dependencies", e);
            throw new InvalidFileException(e);
        }

        return state;
    }

    private <T> T retryOnTransientIO(Supplier<T> action) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        int MAX_RETRIES = 5;
        int WAIT_TIME_MS = 100;

        for (int i = 0; i < MAX_RETRIES; ++i) {
            try {
                return action.get();
            } catch (IOException | InterruptedException e) {
                if (i + 1 == MAX_RETRIES || !isTransient(e)) {
                    throw e;
                }

                Thread.sleep(WAIT_TIME_MS);
                WAIT_TIME_MS *= 2; // Exponential backoff
            }
        }

        return null; // Should never be reached, added to satisfy the compiler
    }

    private boolean isTransient(Exception e) {
        Throwable rootCause = ExceptionUtils.getRootCause(e);
        return (rootCause instanceof InterruptedIOException || 
                rootCause instanceof AsyncTimeoutException);
    }
}
``` 

Please note that this example is a basic skeleton and may require modifications based on your specific requirements and libraries you are using for logging, concurrency, serialization/deserialization, dependency management etc. Also, please consider implementing the following classes: `MoSTState` (to be loaded), `ConfigurationManagementService` to get the dependencies' versions, `DependencyManagementSystem` to resolve the dependencies, and exceptions for invalid file paths, version mismatches and serialization issues. 

