['Here is an example implementation of the task using `reqwest`, `tokio` for async and concurrency management, `mio` for socket handling, `hyper` for HTTP/HTTPS, and `env_logger` for logging. Please note that writing a full solution to your request would be beyond the scope of this platform due to space constraints, but here is a basic starting point:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use reqwest::Url;
use reqwest::Error as ReqwestError;
use hyper::{Body, Request};
use tokio::sync::RwLock;
use env_logger::Env;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Configure logger
    env_logger::init();

    let urls = vec![Url::parse("http://localhost:8080")?, Url::parse("https://example.com")?];
    let client = Client::new(urls);

    loop {
        tokio::spawn({
            let client = client.clone();
            async move {
                match client.get("/api/data").await {
                    Ok(_) => printlnSpaced("Success"),
                    Err(e) => eprintSpaced(&format_error(e)),
                }
            }
        });
        tokio::time::delay_for(std::time::Duration::from_millis(100)).await;
    }
}

struct Client {
    urls: RwLock<HashMap<usize, Url>>,
}

impl Client {
    fn new(urls: Vec<Url>) -> Self {
        let mut map = HashMap::new();
        for (i, url) in urls.into_iter().enumerate() {
            map.insert(i, url);
        }
        Client {
            urls: RwLock::new(map),
        }
    }

    async fn get(&self, path: &str) -> Result<Body, ReqwestError> {
        let url = self.urls.read().await.values().next().unwrap();

        // Create HTTPS client
        let https_client = reqwest::ClientBuilder::new()
            .danger_accept_invalid_certs(true)  // for testing purposes only, not recommended for production
            .build()?;

        // Send GET request
        match https_client.get(&url).send().await {
            Ok(res) => res.body(),
            Err(e) => return Err(e),
        }
    }
}
```
This is a basic example, you would need to expand on this by adding error handling and retries, connection pooling etc. The actual implementation will depend on the specific requirements of your use case. Please also remember that writing an asynchronous HTTP client with all these features in Rust requires a deep understanding of async programming concepts in Rust and how the libraries interact with each other.
