async fn build_project(config_dir: &Path) -> Result<()> {
    let mut configs = HashMap::new();
    for entry in fs::read_dir(config_dir)? {
        let path = entry?.path();
        if !path.is_file() || match path.extension() {
            None => false,
            Some(ext) => ext == "json",
        } {
            continue;
        }

        let contents = fs::read_to_string(&path)?;
        configs.insert(path.file_name().unwrap().to_str().unwrap(), contents);
    }

    // The Mutex is used to ensure only one build process runs at a time
    let mut reports_mutex = Mutex::new(Vec::<BuildReport>::new());
    let start_time = Instant::now();

    let logs: Vec<String>;
    for level in 0..=4 {
        // This will keep retrying until build succeeds or fails permanently.
        match Command::new("cargo")
            .arg("build")
            .arg(format![
                "--verbose"
            ])
            .current_dir(&config_dir)
            .stdout(Stdio::piped())
            .stderr(Stdio::piped())
            .spawn() {
            Ok(mut child) => match child.wait() {
                Ok(_) => break,
                Err(e) => return Err(anyhow::Error::new(e)),
            },
            Err(e) => return Err(anyhow::Error::new(e)),
        }
    }

    let usage = tokio::process::ProcessExt::memory_usage().await.unwrap();
    let duration = start_time.elapsed().as_secs();

    // Update the build report with the new information.
    {
        let mut reports = reports_mutex.lock().await;
        let report = BuildReport::default();
        report.resource_usage = (duration, usage);
        report.logs = logs;
        reports.push(report);
    }

    Ok(())
}
``` 

This code assumes a few things:
- It's running on a Unix system that has cargo installed globally.
- The configuration files are in JSON format and contain build information for individual crates.
- The function is being called from an async context, as it uses Tokio for concurrency and file I/O.
- Each call to the function starts a new set of reports. If you want to keep track of all builds over time, you'll need to store these somewhere persistent or expose them through a method on your build system.
- The memory usage is measured using tokio::process::ProcessExt::memory_usage(), which gives the total memory used by the process and all its threads in bytes. This includes heap, stack, and static allocations. It's not possible to measure only Rust heap allocation (which would be a great addition) without adding native code that interacts with OS-specific APIs.
']

['Here's a basic implementation of the `ClinicStatusUpdater` class in PHP using PDO for database interaction, GuzzleHttp client to interact with external APIs, and Monolog library for logging. The service uses a semaphore-like locking mechanism with retry logic for transient database failures.

```php
<?php
use GuzzleHttp\Client;
use PDO;
use Monolog\Logger;
use Monolog\Handler\StreamHandler;

class ClinicStatusUpdater {
    private $db;
    private $apis = []; // array of API endpoints to interact with
    private $lock = false;
    private $logger;

    public function __construct($config) {
        $this->db = new PDO($config['dsn'], $config['username'], $config['password']);
        foreach ($config['apis'] as $api) {
            $this->apis[] = new Client(['base_uri' => $api]);
        }
        $this->logger = new Logger('ClinicStatusUpdater');
        $this->logger->pushHandler(new StreamHandler('/path/to/your.log', Logger::WARNING));
    }

    public function updateStatus($clinicId, $status) {
        while (!$this->lock); // spin until we have the lock
        $this->lock = true; // set our lock

        try {
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare("UPDATE clinics SET status=?, last_modified_ip=? WHERE id=?");
            for ($i = 0; $i < 5; $i++) { // retry logic for transient database failures
                try {
                    $stmt->execute([$status, $_SERVER['REMOTE_ADDR'], $clinicId]);
                    break;
                } catch (\PDOException $e) {
                    $this->logger->error('Database error: '. $e->getMessage());
                }
            }
        } finally {
            $stmt = null;
            $this->lock = false; // release the lock
        }
    }
}
?>
```
This is a basic implementation and does not include caching, load balancing, or any form of optimization for performance. Also note that this service requires proper handling of exceptions and error checking. In a real-world scenario, you would want to handle exceptions properly and provide meaningful responses when things go wrong.
