['Here is a very simplified version of what this code would look like. This example is just for the basic structure, actual implementation can be complex based on specific requirements and constraints as it involves integration with external services which are not demonstrated here. 

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>

// An abstract class for logging service
class LoggingService {
public:
    virtual void log(const std::string& message) = 0;
};

// A concrete implementation of the logging service
class ConsoleLoggingService : public LoggingService {
public:
    void log(const std::string& message) override {
        std::cout << "LOGGING SERVICE: " << message << std::endl;
    }
};

// Forward declaration of MessagingQueue and its concrete implementation
class MessagingQueue;

class ServiceManager {
private:
    std::forward_list<std::string> stringsList;
    LoggingService* loggingService; 
public:
    ServiceManager() : loggingService(new ConsoleLoggingService()) {}
    ~ServiceManager() { delete loggingService; }

    void processAndInsertString(const std::string& search, const std::string& insert) {
        try {
            auto it = stringsList.begin();
            while (it != stringsList.end()) {
                if (*it == search) {
                    // Found the search string, inserting immediately after it
                    it = stringsList.insert_after(it, insert);
                    ++it;
                    break;
                } else {
                    ++it;
                }
            }

            if (it == stringsList.end()) {
                // Append the string to the end of the list
                stringsList.insert_after(stringsList.before_begin(), insert);
            }
        } catch (const std::exception& ex) {
            loggingService->log("An error occurred while processing and inserting string: " + std::string(ex.what()));
        }
    }
};
``` 
This code provides a simple structure for the ServiceManager, which processes and inserts strings into a forward list. Error handling is provided through try/catch blocks and logging is performed by way of an abstract LoggingService class that can be implemented to suit your specific requirements (for example, sending logs to a file, system console or external log aggregation service). 

The MessagingQueue class was not shown but its role would be to send messages to external services when certain events occur. Its implementation details are omitted for simplicity. Note that in a real-world scenario, integration with messaging queues and logging services typically involve asynchronous operations, which adds complexity to the problem.
