['Here is a basic implementation for your `NetworkController` class in C++. Please note that the actual implementation would be much more complex than this and will require extensive knowledge of network programming, threading, synchronization primitives, and security best practices. 

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <thread>
// Include other necessary headers...

class NetworkController {
private:
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    bool ready = false;

public:
    void run() {
        // Initialize and start threads for handling network connections.
        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; ++i) {
            std::thread t(&NetworkController::handleConnection, this);
            t.detach();
        }
    }

    void handleConnection() {
        // Handle incoming network connection here...

        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lck(mtx);
        while (!ready) cv.wait(lck);

        // Continue handling network connection after ready signal received...
    }

    void notifyReady() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lck(mtx);
        ready = true;
        cv.notify_all();
    }
};
```
This code provides a basic illustration of how you might structure your NetworkController class in C++. It uses a condition variable to signal all threads when the system is ready for processing network connections, and uses a mutex for thread safety during this state change. However, this is only a simplified version. A full implementation would need to include more complex error handling, security measures, dynamic configuration, adaptive resource management, and integration with third-party tools.
