["This task requires a substantial amount of code, which would exceed the character limit for this platform. However, I can give you a high-level architecture design and some pseudo-code samples for your requirements. Please note that creating such a framework would require several months to a few years of development time. 

```java
interface LogProcessor {
    void process(Log log);
}

interface Sink {
    void ingest(Log log);
}

class LoggerFramework {
    List<LogProcessor> logProcessors;
    List<Sink> sinks;

    void addProcessor(LogProcessor processor) {...}
    void removeProcessor(LogProcessor processor) {...}

    void addSink(Sink sink) {...}
    void removeSink(Sink sink) {...}

    void log(Log log) {
        for (LogProcessor processor : logProcessors) {
            processor.process(log);
        }

        for (Sink sink : sinks) {
            sink.ingest(log);
        }
    }
}
```

For the plugin system, you might want to use a service loader pattern. This would allow developers to drop in new plugins as JAR files and have them automatically picked up by your framework without requiring any code changes. 

```java
ServiceLoader<LogProcessor> processors = ServiceLoader.load(LogProcessor.class);
for (LogProcessor processor : processors) {
    loggerFramework.addProcessor(processor);
}
```

For the logging deduplication and ordering, you can use a message queue system like Kafka or RabbitMQ to handle high volume of logs. 

Dynamic configuration is typically done by using a dedicated configuration management system (like Spring Cloud Config Server), which allows for real-time changes to be applied without requiring the entire application to restart.

For security, IP whitelisting and request validation would typically be handled in reverse proxy servers or API gateways like NGINX, Envoy etc. 

Rate limiting can also be implemented using these same types of proxies or by implementing it at your LoggerFramework level, where you track the number of requests per IP address or user account and deny further requests if they exceed a certain threshold.

For concurrency issues, consider using Java's built-in `ExecutorService` and `Callable` interfaces to handle high-load scenarios. 

Testing would involve creating unit tests for individual components, integration tests to verify that the framework works with all of its dependencies, stress testing to validate how the system behaves under extreme conditions, and security testing to identify any potential vulnerabilities. 

