['Creating a fully featured framework in this format would require much more than a code block; it requires multiple classes, potentially using advanced C++ features like thread-safety and asynchronous processing, external libraries for networking and disk I/O operations, along with extensive error handling and testing infrastructure. It would also be impractical to provide the full framework here due to its complexity and length. However, I can provide a simplified example of how to start such a logging framework:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <queue>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

class Logger {
public:
    enum class Level { DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR };
    enum class Output { CONSOLE, FILE, NETWORK };

    static Logger& getInstance() {
        static Logger instance;
        return instance;
    }

    void log(Level level, const std::string &message) {
        if (level < output_level) return;

        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(log_mutex);
        switch (output) {
            case Output::CONSOLE:
                std::cout << message << std::endl;
                break;
            case Output::FILE:
                log_file << message << std::endl;
                break;
            // Add network logging here.
        }
    }

    void setLevel(Level level) { output_level = level; }
    void setOutput(Output out, const std::string &filename = "") { 
        if (out == Output::FILE && filename != "") log_file.open(filename);
        else if (out == Output::NETWORK) /* Add network setup here */;
        output = out;
    }

private:
    Logger() : output_level(Level::INFO), output(Output::CONSOLE) {}
    ~Logger() { if (output == Output::FILE) log_file.close(); /* Also close file handle for network logging */}

    std::ofstream log_file;
    Level output_level;
    Output output;
    std::mutex log_mutex;
};
```

This is a very basic example and would need more work to be production-ready. It only supports synchronous logging, does not handle multi-threaded environments in any special way (so you'd need to implement locking manually), and does not provide for extensibility or error handling. 

For asynchronous logging, I recommend using a producer/consumer pattern with an `std::queue` and two threads: one thread for generating logs, the other for consuming them (writing to console/file). For managing external dependencies, consider using smart pointers and RAII for resource management. Finally, consider using libraries like Boost.Log or spdlog to handle complex aspects of logging like filtering, formatting, and destinations.
