["Here's a simple outline of how you can implement this in C# but given the complexity of your requirements, it would be very large scale project. This code doesn't cover all aspects mentioned above but gives a high-level overview of what the system architecture could look like. 

```csharp
public class ConfigurationManager : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ICache cache;
    private readonly ICloudConfigSource cloudConfigSource;
    private bool disposed = false;

    public ConfigurationManager(ICache cache, ICloudConfigSource cloudConfigSource)
    {
        this.cache = cache;
        this.cloudConfigSource = cloudConfigSource;
    }

    public async Task<XmlDocument> GetConfigurationAsync(string key)
    {
        var cachedValue = await cache.GetOrDefaultAsync(key);
        if (cachedValue != null) return cachedValue;

        var config = await cloudConfigSource.GetConfigurationAsync(key);
        await cache.SetAsync(key, config);
        return config;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Dispose(true);
        GC.SuppressFinalize(this);
    }

    protected virtual void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (disposed) return;

        if (disposing) cache?.Dispose();

        disposed = true;
    }
}
```

Here's a basic structure of interfaces `ICache` and `ICloudConfigSource`: 

```csharp
public interface ICache : IDisposable
{
    Task<XmlDocument> GetOrDefaultAsync(string key);
    Task SetAsync(string key, XmlDocument value);
}

public interface ICloudConfigSource
{
    Task<XmlDocument> GetConfigurationAsync(string key);
}
```

You could use libraries like `StackExchange.Redis` for Redis cache implementation and `AWSSDK.SimpleSystemsManagement` or `Azure.Security.KeyVault.Secrets` for integrating with AWS Parameter Store and Azure Key Vault respectively, though the latter two are external APIs so more detailed implementation would be needed based on your actual needs. 

For Kafka messaging system integration you can use `Confluent.Kafka` library. 

For error handling, using proper exception handling techniques and logging mechanisms will be beneficial. You could also consider implementing a fallback strategy when cloud-based config source fails to fetch configurations or Redis cache server is not available.

This code doesn't include distributed NoSQL database integration as it can vary based on your actual requirements and the one you are using. 

Also note that this is a simplified example, real implementation would need much more complexity and careful design considerations regarding error handling, concurrency control, performance optimization, security measures, logging etc. It's also recommended to use an existing library or framework for such complex system which can help with many of these concerns. 

It's always important to balance complexity against the benefits - if your configuration management service is simple and doesn't need to support hundreds of services and large XML files, you might not need all this complexity. But if it does, then a well-designed solution will save you a lot of headaches in the future.
"]

