['Here's a basic implementation of your requirements using ES6 JavaScript. It uses Set to ensure uniqueness and only add scripts that are unique and defined with all necessary attributes. It utilizes Promises for concurrent operations handling, Error Handling, and caching strategies.

```javascript
class ScriptManager {
    constructor() {
        this.scriptCache = new Map(); // Cached scripts map for quick lookups and preventing duplicate requests
        this.apiCallLimit = 10; // Adjust according to rate limiting by the API
        this.activeApiCalls = 0; // To track current active api calls
    }

    renderFooterScripts(scripts) {
        const scriptElements = Array.from(new Set(scripts), (scriptObj) => this._generateScriptTag(scriptObj));
        return Promise.allSettled(scriptElements);
    }

    _authenticateAndFetchScript(src) {
        // Here you would implement your authentication and caching strategy 
        // For the purpose of this exercise, we assume it's handled by a placeholder function named `fetchAuthenticatedScript`.

        return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
            if (this.scriptCache.has(src)) {
                resolve(this.scriptCache.get(src));
            } else if (this.activeApiCalls >= this.apiCallLimit) {
                setTimeout(() => this._authenticateAndFetchScript(src).then(resolve, reject), 50); // Rate limiting
            } else {
                this.activeApiCalls++;
                fetchAuthenticatedScript(src).then((data) => {
                    this.scriptCache.set(src, data);
                    this.activeApiCalls--;
                    resolve(data);
                }).catch((error) => {
                    this.activeApiCalls--;
                    reject(error);
                });
            }
        });
    }

    _generateScriptTag({ type, async, defer, src }) {
        if (typeof src !== 'string' || !src) throw new Error('Invalid script source');

        return this._authenticateAndFetchScript(src).then((data) => {
            let tag = `<script ${type ? `type="${type}"` : ''} ${async ? 'async' : ''} ${defer ? 'defer' : ''}>`;
            tag += data.replace(/<\/*script[^>]*>/g, ""); // Remove any script tags in the response to prevent XSS attacks 
            return `${tag}</script>`;
        }).catch((error) => {
            console.error(`Failed to fetch or authenticate script: ${src}, error: ${error}. Returning empty string as fallback.`);
            return ''; // Fallback strategy in case of failure
        });
    }
}
```
This code uses a class `ScriptManager` to handle all the operations related to script generation and caching. It ensures that scripts are fetched and cached only once, and provides error handling for corrupted or invalid script objects. The rate limiting mechanism is implemented so as not to exceed API request limits. 

Please note this code assumes you have a function named `fetchAuthenticatedScript` which handles the authentication and caching strategy for your scripts. This is omitted in this basic example but should be replaced with your actual implementation. 

Also, please be aware that while this solution ensures all scripts are unique and only added once by using a Set, it does not ensure that they're inserted into the HTML document uniquely. If multiple objects have the same script source URL, the script tag will still appear in the HTML multiple times due to how Sets handle duplicate values.
']

