However, I can give you a starting point on how you could start building it:

```rust
use std::collections::VecDeque;
use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};

fn filter_transactions(age: u64, num: usize, weight: f32) -> (f32, String) {
    let mut metrics = Metrics::new();
    let mut transactions = VecDeque::new();

    // Fetch transactions from your storage service
    let fetched_transactions = fetch_transactions(age, num);
    transactions.extend(fetched_transactions);

    let now = SystemTime::now().duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH).unwrap().as_secs();
    let mut max_weighted_sum = 0.0;
    let mut transaction_id = String::new();

    while transactions.len() > 0 {
        let transaction = transactions.pop_front().unwrap();
        let age = now - transaction.timestamp;

        if age < age && transactions.len() < num {
            continue;
        }

        metrics.increment(transaction);
        let weighted_sum = transaction.value * weight;
        if weighted_sum > max_weighted_sum {
            max_weighted_sum = weighted_sum;
            transaction_id = transaction.txn_id;
        }
    }

    metrics.update(max_weighted_sum, age);

    (max_weighted_sum, transaction_id)
}
```

Please note that this is a simplified version and doesn't cover all the points you asked for. A full implementation would require several thousand lines of code and be significantly more complex than this, with handling network errors, service outages, concurrent operations, background tasks etc. 

Moreover, Rust is a low-level language that provides great control over system resources but at the same time it requires careful management to avoid memory leaks or other issues. Writing such code without prior knowledge of Rust can lead to several pitfalls and hard-to-spot bugs. I strongly recommend learning Rust before attempting such project, there are many online resources for that.
"]

