['Here is a simplified version of what a modular logging framework in C might look like. It's important to note that this is just a skeleton code and doesn't include all the functionalities mentioned above. Implementing such a system is complex and requires careful planning, design, testing, and optimization to ensure robustness, security, and efficiency.

```C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef enum { DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR } LogLevel;

static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static char* logBuffer[1024]; // This is a simplified example. In production code, you'd likely use a dynamic data structure like a circular buffer or linked list.
static int logCount = 0; 

void logMessage(LogLevel level, const char* message) {
    if (!message) return;
    time_t t = time(NULL);
    struct tm *tmp = localtime(&t);
    char timestamp[64];
    strftime(timestamp, sizeof(timestamp), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S", tmp);

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (logCount < 1024) {
        logBuffer[logCount++] = malloc(strlen(timestamp) + strlen(message) + 3); // +3 for the space, null terminator and newline
        sprintf(logBuffer[logCount-1], "[%s] %s: %s\n", timestamp, level == DEBUG ? "DEBUG" : (level == INFO ? "INFO" : (level == WARN ? "WARN" : "ERROR")), message);
    } else {
        printf("Log buffer is full. Discarding log message.\n");
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code provides a simple logging mechanism with thread-safety and efficient memory usage. Each log entry is prefixed by its timestamp and level, forming a single string that's then appended to the `logBuffer` array. If the buffer becomes full, it starts discarding logs. 

