```c
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <string.h>
#define AES_256_CBC "AES-256-CBC"

// Structure to hold algorithm details
typedef struct {
    const char* name; // Name of the algorithm
    bool enabled;     // Whether this algorithm is active or not
} AlgorithmDetails;

// Array to hold all available algorithms
static AlgorithmDetails g_algorithms[] = { 
    { AES_256_CBC, false },
    /* Add more here */
};

// Returns true if an algorithm is supported and enabled, false otherwise.
bool IsAlgorithmSupportedAndEnabled(const char* name) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof(g_algorithms)/sizeof(g_algorithms[0]); ++i) {
        AlgorithmDetails* details = &g_algorithms[i];
        if (strcmp(details->name, name) == 0 && details->enabled) {
            return true; // Found and enabled.
        }
    }
    return false; // Not found or not enabled.
}

// Function to add an algorithm 
void AddAlgorithm(const char* name, bool enabled) {
    size_t index = sizeof(g_algorithms)/sizeof(g_algorithms[0]);
    ++index;
    g_algorithms = (AlgorithmDetails*)realloc(g_algorithms, 
                                             index * sizeof(AlgorithmDetails));
    AlgorithmDetails* details = &g_algorithms[index-1];
    details->name = name;
    details->enabled = enabled;
}

// Function to initialize the algorithms
void InitializeAlgorithms() {
    OpenSSL_add_all_digests();  // Load all digest methods.
    for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof(g_algorithms)/sizeof(g_algorithms[0]); ++i) {
        AlgorithmDetails* details = &g_algorithms[i];
        EVP_add_cipher(details->name); // Register the cipher.
    }
}
```
The above C code provides a basic structure for managing and initializing cryptographic algorithms using OpenSSL library, allowing users to enable or disable different algorithms based on their availability and security requirements. 

To implement this in Java, you would have to use the Bouncy Castle API which is similar to OpenSSL and allows dynamic loading of algorithms in a way that's compatible with your existing infrastructure.

```java
import org.bouncycastle.jce.provider.BouncyCastleProvider;
import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import java.security.Security;

public class CryptographicManager {
    public void initializeAlgorithms() {
        Security.addProvider(new BouncyCastleProvider());
    }

    public boolean isAlgorithmSupportedAndEnabled(String name) {
        return Arrays.asList(Security.getProviders()).stream().anyMatch(provider -> provider.getName().equals("BC") && 
                Stream.of(provider.getServices()).flatMap(service -> Stream.of(service)).anyMatch(algorithm -> 
                        algorithm.contains(name) && ((KeyFactory.getInstance(name, provider).getProvider() == provider) || 
                                (Signature.getInstance(name, provider).getProvider() == provider))));
    }
}
```
The above Java code provides a basic structure for managing and initializing cryptographic algorithms using Bouncy Castle API, allowing users to enable or disable different algorithms based on their availability and security requirements. 

