['This is a very complex task that involves creating a logging framework from scratch in C++ with specific features and requirements. It's beyond the scope of this platform to create a complete solution but I can provide you an outline for a possible start based on your description. 

Here's a simplified example of what it could look like:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <atomic>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <queue>

class Logger {
public:
    // Log levels enum
    enum class Level { DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR };

    // Destinations enum
    enum class Destination { CONSOLE, FILE, NETWORK };

private:
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    std::queue<std::pair<Level, std::string>> queue;
    Level logLevel = Level::INFO;
    Destination destination = Destination::CONSOLE;

public:
    void setLogLevel(Level level) {
        // Set the new log level
        logLevel = level;
    }

    void setDestination(Destination dest) {
        // Set the new destination
        destination = dest;
    }

    template <typename... Args>
    void asyncLog(Level level, const char* format, Args&&... args) {
        if (level >= logLevel) {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

            // Format the message
            int size = snprintf(nullptr, 0, format, std::forward<Args>(args)...) + 1; 
            if (size < 0) {
                throw std::runtime_error("Error during log formatting");
            }

            std::unique_ptr<char[]> buffer(new char[size]);
            snprintf(buffer.get(), size, format, std::forward<Args>(args)...);

            // Push the message to the queue
            queue.push({ level, std::string(buffer.get()) });

            lock.unlock();
            cv.notify_one();
        }
    }

private:
    void processQueue() {
        while (true) {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            cv.wait(lock, [this] { return !queue.empty(); });

            auto log = queue.front();
            queue.pop();
            lock.unlock();

            // Output the message to the appropriate destination
            switch (destination) {
                case Destination::CONSOLE: std::cout << log.second; break;

                // TODO: Implement FILE and NETWORK destinations

                default: throw std::runtime_error("Invalid destination");
            }
        }
    }
};
```
This is a very simplified example, but it should give you an idea of how to start implementing this. Please note that this does not include all the features you mentioned (such as error handling, dynamic configuration, integration with external systems via gRPC or HTTP/REST, performance optimization for high-frequency logging scenarios, concurrency and thread safety, resource management, monitoring and debugging tools, security etc.) 

For each of these features, it would require a significant amount of additional code, possibly involving more advanced C++ concepts like templates, specialization, inheritance, memory management techniques, network programming (for the networking destination), etc. 

This is not something that can be done in few lines and you should consider hiring professional developers if you want a robust, production-ready solution.
']

