["The task you are asking for is quite complex and would require a lot of code to cover all the requirements in detail. However, I'll give a very high-level example on how one might begin building such a pipeline using Java. 

```Java
public class DataProcessingPipeline {
    private SchemaRegistry schemaRegistry;
    private NoSQLDatabase noSqlDb;
    private List<DataTransformationRule> rules;
    private BackpressureMechanism backpressureMech;
    private MonitoringSystem monitorSys;
    private MetricsInsights metricsInsight;
    private ThreadPoolExecutor executorService;

    public DataProcessingPipeline(SchemaRegistry schemaRegistry, NoSQLDatabase noSqlDb, List<DataTransformationRule> rules, 
            BackpressureMechanism backpressureMech, MonitoringSystem monitorSys, MetricsInsights metricsInsight, ThreadPoolExecutor executorService) {
        this.schemaRegistry = schemaRegistry;
        this.noSqlDb = noSqlDb;
        this.rules = rules;
        this.backpressureMech = backpressureMech;
        this.monitorSys = monitorSys;
        this.metricsInsight = metricsInsight;
        this.executorService = executorService;
    }

    public void processData(JSONEventMessage event) {
        schemaRegistry.validateAndParse(event);

        for (DataTransformationRule rule : rules) {
            event = rule.apply(event);
        }

        noSqlDb.storeData(event);

        backpressureMech.handleBackPressure();
        monitorSys.monitorSystems();
        metricsInsight.generateMetrics();
    }

    public void startProcessing() {
        while (true) { // for simplicity, we assume the data comes in a stream
            JSONEventMessage event = readDataFromStream();
            executorService.submit(() -> processData(event));
        }
    }
}
```
This is a very high-level and simplified example of what your pipeline might look like. In reality, each component (SchemaRegistry, NoSQLDatabase, etc.) would need to be implemented in a more detailed manner and the backpressure mechanism would likely involve some sort of queuing or throttling strategy depending on how you choose to handle spikes in event rates. Also, remember that this is a simple example and doesn't include many aspects such as error handling, logging, security, etc. 

