["Here is a simplified version of the code required to create a distributed file synchronization service in Java which meets your requirements. Please note that this code doesn't include all the features mentioned (such as conflict resolution, indexing system, RESTful API for administration etc). It also lacks certain design principles like separation of concerns and encapsulation.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import com.google.common.cache.*;

public class FileSynchronizationService {
    private final LoadingCache<Path, byte[]> cache;
    private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    public FileSynchronizationService() {
        this.cache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
            .maximumSize(1_000)  // Adjust size as per your need
            .expireAfterWrite(5, TimeUnit.MINUTES) // Adjust time as per your need
            .removalListener((RemovalNotification<Path, byte[]> notification) -> {
                executor.submit(() -> saveToDisk(notification.getKey(), notification.getValue()));
            })
            .build(new CacheLoader<Path, byte[]>() {
                @Override
                public byte[] load(Path key) throws Exception {
                    return readFromDisk(key);
                }
            });
    }

    private void saveToDisk(Path path, byte[] data) {
        try (OutputStream output = new BufferedOutputStream(new FileOutputStream(path.toFile()))) {
            output.write(data);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new UncheckedIOException(e);
        }
    }

    private byte[] readFromDisk(Path path) throws IOException {
        return Files.readAllBytes(path);
    }
}
```
In this code, we use Guava's `LoadingCache` to cache files in memory. When the cache exceeds its maximum size or a file is not accessed for a while, it gets evicted and saved to disk on a separate thread.

Please note that implementing all of these features from scratch would be very complex and time-consuming. It's better to use existing solutions like Apache Hadoop or distributed filesystems like Ceph or GlusterFS which are designed for exactly this purpose.
