["This problem is too complex to fully solve in this format but I'll provide a high-level skeleton of how such an application can be designed, focusing on the main components you mentioned. This is a simplified example and doesn't include all the features you listed but it should give you a starting point. 

Note: C++ is not typically used for financial applications due to its performance characteristics. However, if that was a requirement we could design our solution in a language more suited for high-performance computing like Rust or Go.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <unordered_map>

// Assume we have a DataProvider class to interface with external data sources
class DataProvider { /*...*/ }; 

// Assume we have an EventBus class to handle communication with clients
class EventBus { /*...*/ }; 

class Application {
public:
    Application(std::vector<DataProvider> providers) : providers_(providers) {}

    void start() {
        for (auto& provider : providers_) {
            threads_.push_back(std::thread(&Application::process, this, std::ref(provider)));
        }
    }

    void stop() {
        stopped_ = true;
        cv_.notify_all();
        for (auto& thread : threads_) {
            if (thread.joinable()) {
                thread.join();
            }
        }
    }

private:
    std::vector<DataProvider> providers_;
    std::mutex mtx_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
    bool stopped_ = false;
    std::vector<std::thread> threads_;
    EventBus eventBus_;

    void process(DataProvider& provider) {
        while (true) {
            if (stopped_) return;

            // Retrieve data from the provider
            auto data = provider.get_data();

            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
            cv_.wait(lock, [this] { return stopped_; });

            // Send data to clients via event bus
            eventBus_.publish(data);
        }
    }
};
```

In this example, each `DataProvider` runs on its own thread and retrieves data from an external source. This data is then published over the `EventBus` for consumption by subscribed clients. The application can be stopped cleanly using a condition variable to notify all threads to stop processing data.

This solution does not include many features you listed like data validation, error recovery or dynamic adaption, this would need additional complexity and consideration outside the scope of this example. It also does not consider distributed system concepts such as partitioning and replication. 

It's worth noting that designing a robust C++ application with these requirements is complex and requires a significant amount of time and expertise in C++, concurrency, networking and system design. A more appropriate language for such an application might be Java or Go (especially if the data volume is expected to be very high).
