["This task is complex and involves a lot of considerations and design decisions that can't be fully covered in a single code block due to the size and complexity of such a system. However, I will provide a simplified version which meets your criteria of managing a linked list dynamically with thread safety. This example assumes you have access to a POSIX compatible system:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <assert.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    // ... your structure definition here ...
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

typedef struct LinkedList {
    Node* head;
    Node* tail;
    size_t length;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
} LinkedList;

LinkedList* create_linkedlist() {
    LinkedList* list = malloc(sizeof(LinkedList));
    assert(list != NULL);

    list->head = list->tail = NULL;
    list->length = 0;
    pthread_mutex_init(&list->lock, NULL);

    return list;
}

void append_to_linkedlist(LinkedList* list, NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU data) {
    assert(list != NULL);

    Node* node = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    assert(node != NULL);

    node->data = data;
    node->next = NULL;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&list->lock);

    if (list->tail == NULL) {
        list->head = list->tail = node;
    } else {
        list->tail->next = node;
        list->tail = node;
    }

    list->length++;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&list->lock);
}
```
In this example, we have a `LinkedList` structure that contains the head and tail of our linked list. We also maintain a length field for fast size lookup and use a mutex to ensure thread safety when adding new elements. 

You'll need to augment this with error handling, persistence to an external database, memory pool management, logging, performance metrics, cleanup mechanisms, real-world deployment scenarios, documentation of assumptions, constraints, etc. The design and implementation would be much more complex and extensive based on the specific requirements. 

Also, please note that C language itself is not thread safe and you must use synchronization mechanisms like mutexes to ensure correct behavior in multi-threaded applications. It's crucial to understand how these mechanisms work and choose them wisely to avoid potential problems like deadlocks or priority inversion.
