['This task requires multiple components such as creating an MD5 hash generator, configuring a thread pool, designing a machine learning model, connecting with databases, setting up security measures, handling exceptions, and parsing file contents. 

Due to the complexity of this task, I'll provide a high-level overview of how you might tackle each component:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use md5::{Digest, Md5};
use tokio::sync::Mutex;
use tokio::task::spawn_blocking;
use std::time::SystemTime;
use serde::Serialize;

#[derive(Debug, Serialize)]
pub struct Record {
    pub hash: String,
    pub accesses: usize,
    last_accessed: SystemTime,
}

type Records = Mutex<HashMap<String, Record>>;

async fn get_hash(input: String, records: &Records) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let record = match records.lock().await.get(&input) {
        Some(record) => {
            spawn_blocking(move || {
                let mut record = record.clone();
                record.accesses += 1;
                record.last_accessed = SystemTime::now();
                *records.lock().await.get_mut(&input).unwrap() = record;
            }).await?;
            Some(record.clone())
        },
        None => {
            let mut hasher = Md5::new();
            hasher.update(input);
            let hash = format![for c in hasher.finalize_reset()]format!"{c:02x}"];
            spawn_blocking(move || {
                records.lock().await.insert(input, Record {
                    hash,
                    accesses: 1,
                    last_accessed: SystemTime::now(),
                });
            }).await?;
            None
        },
    };

    println![Record for "{}": {:#?}", input, record];

    Ok(())
}
```
This is just a simplified example to show how you might approach creating and accessing `record` structures containing hashes, access frequency metadata, and timestamps. A full implementation would need more considerations such as database integration, security measures, interface design for external cryptographic libraries, load balancing strategies, and much more.

Note: The actual solution may vary depending on the specific requirements of your application.
