['Here's an example of how the library might look in Java. This is a high-level design with a lot of details omitted for simplicity:

```java
public class AsyncHttpLibrary {
    private int maxConcurrentRequests;
    private RequestQueue requestQueue;
    private ThreadPool threadPool;
    private ConnectionPool connectionPool;
    private ErrorHandler errorHandler;
    private AdaptiveScaler adaptiveScaler;
    private Logger logger;

    public AsyncHttpLibrary(int maxConcurrentRequests) {
        this.maxConcurrentRequests = maxConcurrentRequests;
        this.requestQueue = new RequestQueue();
        this.threadPool = new ThreadPool(maxConcurrentRequests);
        this.connectionPool = new ConnectionPool();
        this.errorHandler = new ErrorHandler();
        this.adaptiveScaler = new AdaptiveScaler(this);
        this.logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(AsyncHttpLibrary.class);
    }

    public void addRequest(Request request) {
        requestQueue.enqueue(request);
    }

    public void startProcessing() {
        new Thread(() -> {
            while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
                try {
                    Request request = requestQueue.dequeue();
                    threadPool.execute(() -> processRequest(request));
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            }
        }).start();
    }

    private void processRequest(Request request) {
        HttpURLConnection connection = null;
        try {
            // Retrieve a connection from the connection pool
            connection = connectionPool.getConnection(request);

            errorHandler.executeWithRetries(() -> executeHttpRequest(connection, request));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.error("Error processing request", e);
        } finally {
            if (connection != null) {
                // Return the connection to the pool after use
                connectionPool.returnConnection(connection);
            }
        }
    }

    private void executeHttpRequest(HttpURLConnection connection, Request request) throws Exception {
        // Execute HTTP request using the provided connection and handle errors accordingly
    }
}
```

The library is organized around several key components: a thread pool for executing requests, a connection pool for managing connections to external resources, an error handler for implementing retry logic with exponential backoff, an adaptive scaler for dynamically adjusting the maximum number of concurrent requests based on system load and performance data, and a logger for capturing detailed information about request processing.

Please note that this is just a skeleton code and not a complete working example. You'll have to implement all those components, which are quite complex and involve many aspects such as network programming, concurrent programming, error handling, etc. Also please consider the library is expected to be used by multiple threads so you may need to take thread safety into account for all methods.
