["Implementing such a robust logging framework requires deep knowledge of C++ programming concepts like classes, templates, namespaces, etc., as well as familiarity with external libraries and services for logging. This task will be challenging due to the complexity involved in designing an adaptive, dynamic, secure, and reliable system that integrates with multiple services under high-load conditions. Below is a simplified example of how you could structure a portion of such a framework. Please note that this code doesn't include all the features mentioned and it will require significant work to turn it into a production-ready software.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>

namespace logging {
    class Logger {
        std::vector<Service*> services;
        std::mutex mtx; // used for thread safety.

      public:
        void log(const char* msg) {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); 

            Service* chosen = choose();

            if (chosen != nullptr) {
                // Try to send the log message, handle potential failures gracefully.
                chosen->sendLog(msg);
            } else {
                // If all services are down or there is no service available, fallback to local logging.
                fallback();
            }
        }

      private:
        Service* choose() {
            std::sort(services.begin(), services.end(), [](const Service& a, const Service& b) {
              return a.getReliabilityScore() > b.getReliabilityScore();  // Choose the service with highest reliability score.
            });

            for (auto &service : services) {
                if (!service->isDown()) {
                    return service;
                }
            }

            return nullptr;  // Return nullptr if all services are down.
        }

        void fallback() {
            // Implement a local logging mechanism or use another available service for fallback.
        }
    };
}
```
In this example, `Service` class represents an external service like cloud-based logging service such as AWS Cloudwatch, Google Stackdriver Logging, etc. Each of these services can have its own reliability score based on certain metrics like response time, availability, etc. The framework will periodically check the status of all services and choose the most reliable one that is not down. If all services are unavailable, it will fallback to a local logging mechanism. 

This is just a simplified example and in real world applications you would need more robust error handling mechanisms, secure communication with services, compliance management for legal requirements, adaptive capacity planning, graceful degradation strategies, etc., which require additional coding and thorough design consideration.
"]

