['Designing such a microservice in JavaScript and C++ requires careful planning, design, and implementation. Here are some general guidelines that you can follow to create this service. 

```javascript
// A JavaScript function to safely execute a shell command using a child process
function safeShellCommand(command) {
    const spawn = require('child_process').spawn;
    const childProcess = spawn('/bin/sh', ['-c', command]);

    let error = "";
    childProcess.stderr.on('data', (data) => { error += data });

    childProcess.stdout.on('data', (data) => { console.log(`stdout: ${data}`); });

    childProcess.on('close', (code) => {
        if(error !== ""){ 
            console.log(`child process exited with error code ${code}: ${error}`);
        } else {
            console.log(`child process exited with code ${code}`);
        }
    });
}
```

The above JavaScript function uses the child_process module to spawn a new shell and execute a command. It captures any error output from stderr, logs it, and then prints the exit code of the child process when it finishes executing.

And in C++, we can create a semaphore using the standard library functions:

```cpp
#include <semaphore>
#include <iostream>

std::counting_semaphore<1> sem(1); // Initialize the semaphore with an initial value of 1

void useSemaphore() {
    sem.acquire(); // Acquire the semaphore

    std::cout << "Access to resource granted." << std::endl;

    // ... Perform operations on shared resources here

    sem.release(); // Release the semaphore
}
```
In this C++ code, we first create a counting_semaphore with an initial value of 1 (representing whether or not access to some resource is currently being used). We then define a function `useSemaphore` that acquires and releases the semaphore around operations on shared resources, ensuring that no two threads can use the resource simultaneously.
']

