['Below is an outline for the C++ RNG library implementation considering all your mentioned requirements. This is not runnable code but a high-level blueprint on how to approach this task:

```cpp
#include <random>
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>
#include <thread>

// Define a thread local random number generator for each thread
thread_local std::mt19937 rng(std::random_device{}());

class RNG {
public:
    // Constructor to accept configuration options
    explicit RNG(int numThreads, int minLimit = 0, int maxLimit = INT_MAX) 
        : numThreads_(numThreads), minLimit_(minLimit), maxLimit_(maxLimit) {

        if (numThreads <= 0 || maxLimit <= minLimit || maxLimit < 0 || minLimit < 0) 
            throw std::invalid_argument("Invalid parameters");
    }

    // Generate a sequence of random integers in the range [min,max]
    std::vector<int> generateRandomSequence(int size) {
        if (size <= 0) throw std::invalid_argument("Invalid size");

        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_); // Ensure thread safety

        std::uniform_int_distribution<> distr(minLimit_, maxLimit_);
        std::vector<int> sequence(size);
        for (auto &num : sequence) 
            num = distr(rng);

        return sequence;
    }

private:
    int numThreads_;
    int minLimit_;
    int maxLimit_;

    std::mutex mutex_; // For thread safety
};
```
The code above outlines a basic multi-threaded random number generator. It uses the `std::mt19937` for each thread to ensure thread safety and independence across different execution contexts, with `<random>` providing a high-performance RNG engine. The class manages its own mutex (mutual exclusion) ensuring thread safety and shared resource access is synchronized.

This code needs further modifications according to all the requirements mentioned above. For example, error handling, scalability and integration testing, documentation, memory management, configuration options, etc., should be added. Also, for a full-featured RNG library with integration into an existing system, it's more complex than the sample provided here.
