["The task you're asking to be done involves creating a complex system with many intricacies. Here is a simplified version of what the library code might look like:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <future>

// A simple thread-safe queue for demonstration purposes only.
template<typename T>
class ThreadSafeQueue {
    std::queue<T> data;
    mutable std::mutex m;
public:
    void push(T new_value) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
        data.push(new_value);
    }

    bool try_pop(T& value) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
        if (data.empty())
            return false;
        value = data.front();
        data.pop();
        return true;
    }
};

class KeyGenerator {
private:
    ThreadSafeQueue<std::packaged_task<int()>> work_queue;
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
public:
    KeyGenerator(size_t num_workers) : stop(false) {
        try {
            for (size_t i = 0; i < num_workers; ++i) {
                threads.push_back(std::thread(&KeyGenerator::workerThread, this));
            }
        } catch (...) {
            stop = true;
            throw;
        }
    }

    ~KeyGenerator() {
        stop = true;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < threads.size(); ++i) {
            if (threads[i].joinable())
                threads[i].join();
        }
    }

    // Submit a task to be performed asynchronously.
    std::future<int> generateKey(std::packaged_task<int()> task) {
        std::packaged_task<int()> pt(task);
        std::future<int> res = pt.get_future();
        work_queue.push(std::move(pt));
        return res;
    }

private:
    void workerThread() {
        while (!stop) {
            std::packaged_task<int()> task;
            if (work_queue.try_pop(task)) {
                task();  // Perform the task in a thread-safe manner.
            } else {
                std::this_thread::yield();
            }
        }
    }
};
```

This is a very basic example and does not actually generate keys, but it demonstrates how to spawn worker threads that process tasks submitted asynchronously with the `std::future`/`std::promise` mechanism. The actual implementation would involve integrating with an external service for key generation which could be done using appropriate libraries or APIs provided by this service.

Note: Writing a fully-featured library to manage keys and perform cryptographic operations is a significant undertaking that requires careful design and rigorous testing. This example just scratches the surface of the complexity involved in such a task. The key components like error handling, logging, latency optimization, and security considerations should be thoroughly implemented to ensure robustness and reliability.
"]

