['Designing such a complex library would require significant time and resources, and is beyond the capabilities of this platform. However, I can provide you with an example of how to use Rust to compile another Rust binary with hooks for measuring compilation time and memory usage:

```rust
use std::time::{Instant};
use std::process::Command;
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::prelude::*;
use std::path::Path;

fn compile_and_measure(src_file: &str, output_bin: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let start = Instant::now();

    // Compile the Rust binary using rustc command
    Command::new("rustc")
        .arg(src_file)
        .arg("-o")
        .arg(output_bin)
        .spawn()?
        .wait()?;

    let duration = start.elapsed();

    // Measure memory usage using /proc filesystem on Linux
    let mut mem_usage: u64 = 0;
    let path = format!["/proc/{}/status", std::process::id()];
    let mut file = File::open(path)?;
    let mut contents = String::new();
    file.read_to_string(&mut contents)?;
    for line in contents.lines() {
        if line.starts_with("VmRSS:") {  // Resident Set Size, corresponds to physical memory used by the process
            let parts = line[7..].trim().split_whitespace().collect::<Vec<&str>>();
            mem_usage = parts[0].parse::<u64>()?;
        }
    }

    println_string::<<format![Compilation time: {} sec, Memory usage: {} kB", duration.as_secs(), mem_usage]>>;

    Ok(())
}
```

This is a simplified example that doesn't cover many aspects of your request such as threading, external data source integration, error handling and edge case scenarios, etc. You would need to expand on this code significantly for an actual library like the one you described. The actual implementation will involve using Rust's powerful features like ownership and lifetimes for memory safety, asynchronous programming with async/await for high performance, and a combination of low-level system programming primitives for efficient resource management.
