['Designing a full implementation in text form is quite difficult as it requires many files and packages. But I can provide a simple Node.js file upload service using express-fileupload and multer for handling multipart/form-data, which are middleware modules for Express and body-parser respectively. 

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const fileUpload = require('express-fileupload');
const app = express();

// enable files upload
app.use(fileUpload({
    createParentPath: true,
    debug: false
}));

// Upload endpoint
app.post('/upload', async (req, res) => {
    try {
        if(!req.files) {
            res.send({status: false, message: 'No file uploaded'});
        } else {
            let uploadFile = req.files.file;

            // Use the mv() method to place the file in upload directory (i.e. "uploads")
            uploadFile.mv(`./uploads/${uploadFile.name}`);

            res.send({status: true, message: 'File is uploaded', data: {name: uploadFile.name, mimetype: uploadFile.mimetype, size: uploadFile.size}});
        }
    } catch (err) {
        console.log(err);
        res.status(500).send(err);
    }
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server started on port 3000'));
```
This is a basic version and doesn't include any error handling or logging mechanism for retries and network failures. It also does not scale horizontally or vertically. For actual production use, you would want to consider using AWS S3 SDK or another cloud service's API to upload files rather than local storage.

