['Implementing all these features of a C library can be quite complex and involves multiple components such as threads, I/O operations, dynamic memory management, data serialization/deserialization, and networking. Moreover, the detailed requirements provided do not provide specific tasks or features that can be implemented in isolation. However, here is an example of how to create a simple thread-safe queue in C:

```C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct Node {
    int value;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node *head = NULL;
Node *tail = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t headLock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_mutex_t tailLock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void enqueue(int value) {
    Node *tmp = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    tmp->value = value;
    tmp->next = NULL;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&tailLock);
    if (tail == NULL) {
        head = tail = tmp;
    } else {
        tail->next = tmp;
        tail = tmp;
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&tailLock);
}

int dequeue() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&headLock);
    Node *tmp = head;
    int result = -1;
    if (tmp != NULL) {
        if (tmp->next == NULL) tail = tmp->next;
        else head = tmp->next;
        result = tmp->value;
        free(tmp);
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&headLock);
    return result;
}
```
This is a simple thread-safe queue where nodes are added to the tail and removed from the head. It uses two mutexes to ensure thread safety, one for protecting the `head` pointer and another one for protecting the `tail` pointer. 

